  "preview": "Six everyday pricing situations. Some are proportional. Some look like they should be — but aren't. Sort them and defend why.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Distinguish proportional from non-proportional relationships in real contexts by checking whether the ratio y:x is constant (and whether the relationship passes through the origin).",
    "studentPrompt": "A student is collecting receipts and bills to study how prices work. For each of the six situations below, decide: is the relationship between the two quantities proportional, or not? Be ready to defend each answer with a ratio, a table, or a graph sketch.\n\nA. A car wash charges $8 per car. The total earned is compared to the number of cars washed.\n\nB. A streaming service charges $4.99 per month, plus a one-time $10 sign-up fee. Total cost vs. number of months.\n\nC. A bag of trail mix is sold by the pound at $6.50/lb. Total cost vs. weight in pounds.\n\nD. A printing company charges $25 for the setup, then $0.40 per shirt. Total cost vs. number of shirts.\n\nE. A bakery sells cookies: 1 cookie for $2.00, 3 cookies for $5.50, 6 cookies for $10.00. Total cost vs. number of cookies.\n\nF. A community pool charges $0 for the first hour and $3 for every hour after that. Total cost vs. number of hours.\n\nFor each situation: (1) Is it proportional? (2) What's your evidence? (3) If it is proportional, what's the constant of proportionality and what does it mean in context?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "Six small receipt-style cards labeled A through F, arranged in two rows on a neutral background, suggesting bills or pricing slips to be sorted.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#f7f3ea\"/><g stroke=\"#3a3a3a\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" fill=\"#ffffff\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"22\" width=\"130\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"175\" y=\"22\" width=\"130\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"330\" y=\"22\" width=\"130\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"118\" width=\"130\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"175\" y=\"118\" width=\"130\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"330\" y=\"118\" width=\"130\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/></g><g font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#3a3a3a\"><text x=\"30\" y=\"40\" font-weight=\"700\">A  Car Wash</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"58\">$8 per car</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"74\">cars: 3</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"90\">total: $24.00</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"40\" font-weight=\"700\">B  Streaming</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"58\">$10 sign-up</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"74\">+$4.99/month</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"90\">total: ?</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"40\" font-weight=\"700\">C  Trail Mix</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"58\">$6.50 / lb</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"74\">weight: 2.4 lb</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"90\">total: $15.60</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"136\" font-weight=\"700\">D  T-Shirts</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"154\">$25 setup</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"170\">+$0.40 each</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"186\">total: ?</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"136\" font-weight=\"700\">E  Bakery</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"154\">1:$2.00  3:$5.50</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"170\">6:$10.00</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"186\">total: ?</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"136\" font-weight=\"700\">F  Pool</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"154\">1st hour free</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"170\">$3 / hour after</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"186\">total: ?</text></g><g stroke=\"#bcb3a0\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\" stroke-width=\"1\"><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"146\" y2=\"96\"/><line x1=\"179\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"301\" y2=\"96\"/><line x1=\"334\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"456\" y2=\"96\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"192\" x2=\"146\" y2=\"192\"/><line x1=\"179\" y1=\"192\" x2=\"301\" y2=\"192\"/><line x1=\"334\" y1=\"192\" x2=\"456\" y2=\"192\"/></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Hand out one card per group to start (or post the six on the board). Say: 'Some of these are proportional, some only look like it. Pick one, prove your case, then trade.' Don't define 'proportional' for them up front — let them argue it out.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: printed cards for each scenario (one per group)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "proportional-reasoning",
      "constant-of-proportionality",
      "unit-rate",
      "linear-vs-proportional",
      "real-world-application"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong sort-and-defend task. Low floor (compute a ratio for one card), high ceiling (E hides a non-proportional surprise; F is sneaky because of the free first hour). Drives the 'must pass through origin' idea naturally.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Groups make a table of (x, y) pairs and check whether y/x is the same every time.\n- Groups sketch a quick graph and look for a straight line through the origin.\n- Strong groups separate 'linear' from 'proportional' — B, D, and F are linear but not proportional because of fixed fees or shifted starts.\n- Some groups will catch that E is NOT proportional because of the cookie discount (3 for $5.50 instead of $6, so the per-cookie rate changes).",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- 'It's proportional because it's linear.' Redirect: ask what the cost is when x = 0. If it isn't $0, the line doesn't pass through the origin.\n- 'F is proportional because it's $3 per hour.' Redirect: check the ratio for 1 hour ($0) vs. 2 hours ($3) — 0/1 and 3/2 are not equal.\n- 'E is proportional because the cookies all have prices.' Redirect: compute y/x for each pair. 2.00/1 = 2.00, 5.50/3 ≈ 1.83, 10.00/6 ≈ 1.67. Not constant.",
    "discussionQuestions": [
      "What's the difference between 'linear' and 'proportional'? Which cards forced you to notice that?",
      "Card F has a clean '$3 per hour' rate after the first hour. Why isn't the whole relationship proportional?",
      "Card E has whole-dollar-ish prices. How could you tell quickly whether it was proportional without doing all the division?"
    ],
    "extensions": [
      "Take card B (streaming, $10 + $4.99/month). Change one thing — drop the sign-up fee — and the relationship becomes proportional. What's the new constant of proportionality? Now go the other direction: keep the fee but change the monthly charge. Can ANY monthly rate make it proportional? Why not?",
      "Card E's bakery offers a 3-for-$5.50 and 6-for-$10.00 discount. Write a piecewise rule for the cost of n cookies (assume the customer always picks the cheapest combo). Is THAT rule proportional? If a customer buys 12 cookies, what's the effective per-cookie price?",
      "Invent a 7th card that LOOKS proportional in a table of three points but isn't. (Hint: pick three (x, y) pairs that are close to but not exactly on a line through the origin.) Trade with a neighbor and try to bust each other's cards."
    ],
    "sampleSolutions": "A. Proportional. y = 8x. k = 8 ($ per car). y/x is always 8.\nB. NOT proportional. y = 10 + 4.99x. At x = 0, y = 10, not 0. Ratios: 14.99/1, 19.98/2 = 9.99, 24.97/3 ≈ 8.32 — not constant.\nC. Proportional. y = 6.50x. k = 6.50 ($ per lb). 15.60 / 2.4 = 6.50. Checks.\nD. NOT proportional. y = 25 + 0.40x. Fixed setup fee breaks the origin.\nE. NOT proportional. Ratios 2.00, 1.833, 1.667 are not constant — bulk pricing changes the rate.\nF. NOT proportional. Free first hour means (1, 0) is on the graph; the line is y = 3(x − 1) for x ≥ 1, which passes through (1, 0) not (0, 0). After the first hour the marginal rate is constant, but the overall relationship isn't proportional.",
